#:blog{:title "Reimagining Muse AI on datom.world: From Opaque VMs to Transparent ASTs"
       :date #inst "2026-09-25T22:20:00.000-00:00"
       :abstract [:p "What happens if you strip Meta's Muse AI out of its black-box 'Secure VM' and rebuild it on datom.world's architectural primitives? We move from trusting a corporation to trusting physics."]
       :content [:section.blog-article
                 [:div.section-inner
                  [:article
                   :$blog-title
                   :$blog-date
                   
                   [:p "Meta's recently announced Muse AI operates on a fundamental Web2 premise: you give the agent your data, and Meta promises to keep it safe. Muse runs in an opaque 'Secure VM' in the cloud. Technologically, this relies on traditional hardware virtualization (often QEMU/KVM) to run a Linux OS, isolating the agent inside an untrusted " [:code "systemd-nspawn"] " container. To prevent the agent from leaking your data, Meta employs kernel-level eBPF monitoring and a heuristic 'Sentinel' guard to watch its every move."]
                   
                   [:p "This model fundamentally relies on 'legal trust'. Even though Meta plans to introduce Confidential VMs (hardware enclaves) in the future, the agent remains a black box running on Meta's infrastructure. You must trust their Privacy Policy, their hardware vendors, and their promise that your interactions will not be shared with ad networks (even though they are used for AI training by default unless you opt out)."]
                   
                   [:p "But what if we completely rebuilt Muse AI using datom.world's architectural primitives? By migrating from a centralized, hardware-level container to a decentralized, mathematically verifiable model using " [:code "yang.compiler"] ", " [:code "yin.vm"] ", " [:code "dao.space"] ", " [:code "dao.jing"] ", and " [:code "shibi"] ", we shift from 'legal trust' to 'structural trust'."]
                   
                   [:h2 "The Reimagined Architecture"]
                   
                   [:h3 "1. The Request (yang.compiler): Intent as Verifiable Code"]
                   [:p "Instead of sending a vague text prompt to a remote server, your intent is passed to a local or remote model which uses the " [:code "yang.compiler"] " to compile its execution plan into a Universal AST (Abstract Syntax Tree). This plan is not an opaque binary payload; it is structured data (datoms) detailing exactly what logic the agent intends to execute."]
                   
                   [:h3 "2. The Sandbox (dao.space & shibi): Data Stays Home"]
                   [:p "Currently, you give Muse OAuth access to pull your Gmail or Spotify data into Meta's QEMU/KVM hypervisor. In the datom.world version, your data never leaves your device. Your local " [:code "dao.space"] " acts as the host. You issue Muse a " [:code "shibi"] " capability token (a cryptographically verifiable Macaroon) with a strict caveat: 'You may read email metadata to find my flight receipt, but you may only return the final flight time; you cannot exfiltrate the email body.'"]
                   
                   [:h3 "3. Execution (yin.vm): Mathematical Confinement"]
                   [:p "Muse pushes its AST to your machine. Before it runs, your machine executes a deterministic Datalog query over the AST itself. If the code tries to express a command like 'Send raw emails to Meta,' the syntax check rejects it. When approved, it runs locally in " [:code "yin.vm"] " (a CESK machine, not an OS emulator). Unlike Meta's Linux containers that require eBPF fences to block network access, " [:code "yin.vm"] " has zero I/O primitives aside from explicitly wired streams. The agent is physically trapped in a vacuum with your data, computing the answer and returning only the safe, attenuated result over the stream."]
                   
                   [:h3 "4. Memory and Audit (dao.jing): Content-Addressed Continuations"]
                   [:p "Every step the agent takes, every variable it binds, and every state change is appended as an immutable datom log into " [:code "dao.jing"] ". The agent's 'memory' isn't a hidden neural state inside a systemd container; it is a fully transparent, queryable database of events."]
                   
                   [:h2 "What this enables (That FB's stack cannot do)"]
                   
                   [:h3 "Zero-Trust Deep Delegation"]
                   [:p "If you want Meta's Muse to analyze your health records, you have to upload that raw data to Meta's servers. Because datom.world uses 'Push-Interpreter-to-Data,' you can let an advanced AI agent analyze your most hyper-sensitive data without the AI's creator ever seeing a single byte of it. The agent is mathematically confined to only return the final aggregate answer."]
                   
                   [:h3 "Time-Travel Debugging & Perfect Auditability"]
                   [:p "If Muse makes a mistake, its internal reasoning is an opaque black box. In contrast, because execution state in " [:code "yin.vm"] " is a log of datoms, you can run a Datalog query against the agent's exact state at the moment it made an error. You can rewind the execution cursor to the moment before a mistake, fix the variable, and let it resume. The agent leaves a mathematically perfect evidence trail."]
                   
                   [:h3 "Agent Roaming (Continuation Transport)"]
                   [:p "Meta's Muse runs in a fixed cloud VM; suspending it requires heavy memory snapshots. In datom.world, because the agent's entire execution state (Control, Environment, Store, Continuation) is just data in " [:code "dao.jing"] ", it can roam frictionlessly. The agent could start a complex task on a cloud server, pause execution, serialize its continuation as datoms, sync them to your phone, and resume the exact same computation locally while you are offline."]
                   
                   [:h3 "Decentralized, Multi-Agent Handoffs"]
                   [:p "Muse acts as a monolithic agent controlled by Meta, where handoffs require cumbersome API calls. In datom.world, an agent written by Meta could compile a sub-task into an AST, attach a scoped " [:code "shibi"] " token, and append it to a stream for a specialized agent written by Google to pick up. Because they both speak Universal AST and datoms, they can seamlessly pass fully-hydrated runtime execution states to one another across the tuple space without custom API integrations."]
                   
                   [:h2 "Summary"]
                   [:p "Facebook's stack is designed to protect the user from the AI by putting the AI in a heavy, guarded OS container. Reimagining Muse on datom.world flips the paradigm: it protects the data from the AI by forcing the AI to become transparent data itself, executing under the strict laws of physics dictated by the user's local machine."]
                   
